  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>|</mo></math></p><p>The given equation represents that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></math> is a point such that it is equidistant from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mo>&amp;</mo><mo> </mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>We know that in a triangle only circumcentre is the point which is equidistant from the three vertices.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0041_914e6feeb525.png\" style=\"width: 323px; height: 194px;\"/><br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is a triangle. Hence its circum-centre will be the only point whose distance from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> will be same.<br/>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>"
  }
